# tests/test_t08_decay_curve.bd - T08 "Meter memory II: decay from
# saturation over seconds, and the ~3.7s throttle constant"
#
# ONE QUESTION
#   Starting from a saturated meter, what does get_cpu() read after
#   exactly k whole seconds of sleeping (k = 0,1,2,3,5,8)? A step down
#   to the floor, or a graded decay?
#
# WHY THIS IS THE SHARPEST OPEN NUMBER IN THE CORPUS
#   Two well-replicated observations flatly contradict each other:
#     (a) test_cpu_decay.bd's idle phase: ONE sleep(1) per iteration is
#         enough for get_cpu() to sit at a rock-steady 10.0, 8 samples
#         running, never climbing. -> the meter clears in under 1s.
#     (b) every real nu.throttle() in the crash traces costs ~3.7s
#         (1 mandatory sleep(1) + ~2.7 retries) before get_cpu() falls
#         back under 80 - replicated ~10 times in one trace, and cross-
#         confirmed by the mid-item-throttle A/B (+3.8s per added call).
#         -> the meter stays above 80 for ~2.7-3.1s after trivial work.
#   Both cannot be true of the same signal. This test measures which,
#   by taking ONE clean sample per delay instead of polling.
#
# THE THREE MODELS, WITH NUMBERS (base tier, floor taken as ~10-20)
#   A. STEP / SHORT WINDOW (<=1s of memory)
#        k=0 ~90-100, and k=1,2,3,5,8 ALL equal at the floor.
#        If A: the 3.7s throttle constant is NOT the meter decaying.
#        It becomes a genuine anomaly needing its own explanation, and
#        every "wait for it to drop" pacing assumption in the codebase
#        is resting on a mechanism that does not exist. Major redirect.
#   B. GEOMETRIC DECAY / EMA (a power-of-two smoothing factor)
#        (reading - floor) falls by a CONSTANT RATIO each second.
#        Signature: successive (post - floor) values divide to the same
#        number. If that ratio is 1/2, 3/4, 7/8, 15/16... the dyadic
#        readings already on record (93.75 = 100*15/16, 99.9984741210938
#        = 100*(1-2^-16), 49.94140625) are explained in one stroke.
#   C. LINEAR DRAIN at ~10 points/second (one base-tier tick's worth)
#        k=0 100, k=1 90, k=2 80, k=3 70, k=5 50, k=8 20 (floor).
#        Signature: successive DIFFERENCES are constant, ~10.
#        This is the model that reproduces the ~3.7s throttle constant
#        exactly (crossing below 80 just after k=2, plus throttle()'s
#        own mandatory first sleep), and it matches the player's live
#        meter, which only ever shows MULTIPLES OF 10.
#   B and C are both "graded"; the corpus has independent evidence for
#   each, so the test reports enough precision to separate them.
#
# HOW TO READ THE RESULT (sort the printed lines BY k, not by phase)
#   all k>=1 equal              -> A. Short window. Redirect the whole
#                                  investigation off "decay" onto
#                                  whatever else makes throttle() cost
#                                  3.7s (T19's job).
#   constant ratio of (post-floor) -> B. Read the ratio off directly;
#                                  it IS the smoothing constant.
#   constant difference ~10     -> C. One tick = 10 points, drained at
#                                  one tick-equivalent per second.
#   post still ~100 at k=8      -> neither. The meter's memory is far
#                                  longer than ANY pacing in this
#                                  codebase assumes. Everything built on
#                                  sleep(1)-clears-it is built on sand.
#   readings track PHASE ORDER instead of k -> not decay at all but
#                                  drift/accumulation over the run; see
#                                  "SCRAMBLED ORDER" below, this is the
#                                  single most important confound and
#                                  the test is built to catch it.
#
# SCRAMBLED ORDER (the design choice that separates decay from drift)
#   Phases run k = 0, 3, 1, 8, 2, 5, 0, 2 - deliberately NOT ascending.
#   If the readings fall monotonically when sorted BY k but scatter when
#   sorted by phase number, the fall is caused by k: real decay.
#   If they fall monotonically by PHASE NUMBER regardless of k, the fall
#   is a session-long trend (long-window memory, warm-up, accumulation)
#   and has nothing to do with the delay - an ascending-k design would
#   have reported exactly the same shape and drawn exactly the wrong
#   conclusion. P7 (k=0 again) and P8 (k=2 again) are literal replicates
#   of P1 and P5 late in the run: if P7~=P1 and P8~=P5, there is no
#   drift and the k-ordering is trustworthy.
#
# THE BURST IS CALIBRATED SUB-LETHAL - AND P1 IS ITS SAFETY CHECK
#   Saturation is 15 unrolled `x = x + 1` lines: ~1.5s of continuous
#   execution at base tier, well inside the 5-sustained-seconds kill.
#   (test_cpu_decay.bd's fatal burst was 50 LOOP ITERATIONS - ~150
#   executed lines, ~15s - which is why it died before measuring
#   anything and why this test is unrolled instead of looped.)
#   15 unrolled lines has never been run, so P1 goes first on purpose:
#   if it is lethal we learn it from the very first phase and the
#   surviving "T08 P1 k=0 begin" line says so unambiguously.
#   `x = x + 1`, not `x = 1`, so a dead-store optimiser cannot elide it.
#
# INSTRUMENT ACCOUNTING (the observer effect is the whole difficulty)
#   - The decay is NOT POLLED. Polling is self-defeating here: every
#     get_cpu() call is a line, every line re-elevates the very quantity
#     being measured - the documented livelock. So each delay gets its
#     own phase with EXACTLY ONE post-burst capture, and every phase
#     carries byte-for-byte identical instrument load. Differences
#     between phases are therefore pure k.
#   - The k delay lines are bare `sleep(1)` - exactly ONE line per
#     second of delay, the minimum possible. A loop would have cost 2-3
#     lines per second, i.e. injected MORE load the longer the delay:
#     precisely the confound this test exists to avoid.
#   - `pre = get_cpu()` before each burst does triple duty: it is the
#     per-phase FLOOR estimate (9 independent samples of it), the
#     contamination check (is 6s of settle actually enough?), and an
#     identical 1-line offset in every phase, so it cancels out.
#   - The result is logged IMMEDIATELY after the capture, not deferred:
#     the log cannot affect a number already taken, and logging at once
#     means a kill can never destroy a measurement that was made.
#   - Every phase opens with 6x sleep(1) of settle. If decay is longer
#     than 6s, `pre` will show it rather than silently poisoning the
#     next burst.
#
# P9 - THE THROTTLE PROBE (the second half of the title)
#   Runs LAST, after every clean phase, so its polling cannot
#   contaminate them. Same 15-line burst, then nu.throttle()'s exact
#   shape inlined - sleep(1), then poll-and-sleep while the reading is
#   above cpu_guard_fast()'s 80 - and reports the total wait.
#   ~3.7s here reproduces the trace constant in isolation for the first
#   time. Compare it against the k at which the UNPOLLED curve crosses
#   80: if the polled wait is much longer than that k, the difference is
#   the poll's own self-inflicted load (the thing T19 decomposes).
#   Bounded at ~14s so it cannot livelock the run; exit_cpu>80 in the
#   output means it timed out, which is itself a headline result.
#
# HOW TO RUN
#   1. Fresh session. Run `ps` and `jobs` first, confirm both empty.
#   2. run test_t08_decay_curve   - then TOUCH NOTHING for ~2 minutes.
#   3. Paste back EVERY "T08 " line, at FULL PRECISION (do not round -
#      the trailing digits are what separate model B from model C).
#   4. If it dies, paste what printed anyway: the last surviving line
#      names the exact phase, and since every phase's burst is
#      identical, a death names 15 unrolled lines as lethal - a fact
#      nothing in the corpus has established.
#   REPEATS: 2 runs, back to back, same session. A 3rd immediately if
#   the two runs disagree on SHAPE (step vs graded) - that disagreement
#   would itself be the headline: the meter is not a deterministic
#   function of elapsed time, which would be the first direct handle
#   anyone has had on the run-to-run variance mystery.
#   RUNTIME: ~2 minutes per run. No imports, no file I/O, no API calls.

var t0 = get_unix_time()
var x = 0
var pre = 0
var post = 0
var c = 0
var tp = 0
log("T08 start - decay from saturation, k = 0,3,1,8,2,5,0,2 - cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))

